  "query_preview": "..A soggy Halloween weekend is expected overall but \nHalloween night is shaping up to be dry in most places. \n\nMoisture continues to surge northward ahead of an approaching trough \nthis afternoon. The trajectory of the moisture combined with some \nshallow instability, will allow for upslope showers to develop and \ncontinue into this afternoon, especially along and south of the \nMogollon Rim. \n\nThe deepening west coast trough will be the catalyst for the next \nround of heavy rainfall from late tonight through Sunday. As we head \ninto this evening, moisture continues to increase and with the mid-\nlevel flow strengthening and rain becoming more widespread across \nthe region. Expect increased activity with moderate to heavy rain to \ncontinue from late tonight into Sunday. On Sunday, the trough starts \nto push to the east. This will drag a strong cold front through the \narea, further enhancing the heavy rain into Sunday evening, plus \nadding a marginal severe risk for Sunday. Main severe hazards will \nbe a damaging wind threat. While conditions are not ideal for \nrotating storms, we cannot rule out an isolated tornado, mainly \nacross the southern most parts of our forecast area. Models continue \nto hit the precipitation aspect of the event hard, especially along \nand south of the Mogollon Rim. PWATs remain well above normal with \nvalues ranging between 1.0\"-1.5\" through much of the region. The \nalready saturated grounds will exacerbate flooding concerns and in \nsome areas we may see sharp rises on creeks and streams with what we \nwould normally consider relatively light rainfall amounts. Rainfall \ntotals remain highest along and south the Mogollon Rim, with totals \nbetween 3\"-6\". Other areas may see 1\"-3\" with locally 4\"+ possible. \nThe Flash Flood Watch remains in effect through late Sunday night \nfor the entire forecast area. It is not often we see a moderate \nexcessive rainfall outlook across our area from the Weather \nPrediction Center, so please heed all warnings and road closures \nthroughout the event. \n\n** There is potential for significant hydrological issues to \ncontinue due to the very wet antecedent conditions. Both flash \nflooding and river flooding will be a problem, with some area \nwaterways remaining elevated for days beyond the main event. Be \nprepared in case you need to take action or need to head to higher \nground. Have a way to receive warnings and always remember, TURN \nAROUND, DON'T DROWN! **\n\nIn addition to the rain, there is the potential for a period of \nstrong, gusty winds from late tonight through Sunday, especially \nalong and east of the Mogollon Rim. The pressure gradient really \ntightens up in advance of the approaching trough and winds may gust \n30-40 mph at times. Event through the winds look to largely remain \nbelow advisory levels, with the very wet conditions some trees may \nbe at risk of uprooting/falling.  \n\nThe trough finally moves out of the area on Monday, with a few \nlingering showers possible across eastern Arizona. Conditions \nquickly dry out but with colder air arriving, some areas may see \nrain turn to snow before all is said and done. Best chances for any \naccumulating snow looks to be across the Kaibab Plateau, San \nFrancisco Peaks, and Buffalo Pass/Chuska Mountains. \n\nA ridge tries to build in behind the departing trough but a low \nremaining off to our north helps to flatten out the flow through the \nmiddle of the week. Overall, looks like to may be a brief period of \nwarming and drying out before the next trough/cur-off low moves \ntowards our area.",
